===Etymology 1===
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:*bangen<ety:inh<ang:*bangian<ety:inh<gem-pro:*bangōną>>>:bor<non:banga>>|tree=1}}
From {{inh|en|enm|*bangen}}, from {{der|en|ang|*bangian}} or borrowed from {{der|en|non|banga|t=to pound, hammer}}; both from {{der|en|gem-pro|*bangōną|t=to beat, pound}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰen-|t=to beat, hit, injure}}. 
{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognate with {{cog|sco|bang}}, {{m|sco|bung|t=to strike, bang, hurl, thrash, offend}}, {{cog|is|banga|t=to pound, hammer}}, {{cog|gmq-osw|bånga}} {{gl|"to hammer"; whence modern {{cog|sv|banka|t=to knock, pound, bang}}}}, {{cog|da|banke|t=to beat}}, {{m|da|bengel|t=club}}, {{cog|nds|bangen}}, {{m|nds|bangeln|t=to strike, beat}}, {{cog|fy|bingel}}, {{m|fy|bongel}}, {{cog|nl|bengel|t=bell; rascal}}, {{cog|de|Bengel|t=club}}, {{m|de|bungen|t=to throb, pulsate}}.
{{col-bottom}}
In the sense of a fringe of hair, from {{m|en|bang off}}.

In the sense of ''abrupt left turn'', from {{w|Boston left}} and associated risk of a crash.

